      "production_promoted": false,
      "hypothesis": "Reserve ngram row capacity but construct rows only on insertion to avoid eagerly touching unused host cache payload.",
      "expected_effect": "Lower startup row initialization while preserving exact BF16 values, mapping and FIFO behavior; evaluate physical memory separately.",
      "controlled_change": "Source-copy lazy row construction with unchanged 158275 slots, 64MiB admitted cache, map reservation, original tables, kernels and model precision.",
      "correctness": "Real prepared tables compare all bytes, logical slots, hit/miss counts, duplicates, EOS history and repeated eviction at small/full capacities. Fresh full-model scratch off/on validation matches the previously qualified eager reference. 38 focused MTP tests pass.",
      "outcome": "Full fixture initializes 564160 row bytes instead of 51914200 after 123 tokens, retaining the full reserve. Short prompt initializes 375232 bytes before decode. Lazy validation-01/02 remain compression-blocked; three diagnostic maps do not identify a compressed owner. Fresh validation-03 and short-01 are clean. No independent eager/lazy speed effect is established.",
      "smallest_experiment": "Keep lazy construction explicit and shared in future controlled experiments. Do not infer that it fixes intermittent compression; require independent lifecycle and request qualification before production adoption.",
      "limitations": [
        "Initialized bytes are not physical footprint.",
        "VM maps may perturb execution and classify regions rather than C++ ownership.",
        "No map timing qualifies speed.",
        "Earlier failed stages remain incomplete."
      ],
